---
import { pacts } from "../../data/coordination.js";
import ChapterImage from "../ChapterImage.astro";
const pact = pacts[0];
---

<section id="race" class="chapter race chapter-portal">
  <ChapterImage
    src="/assets/assembly.webp"
    alt="Conceptual international meeting chamber with an empty circular table and many chairs in soft daylight"
    caption="A shared table. Terms still to be agreed."
  />
  <div class="chapter-heading reveal">
    <div class="section-label">
      <span>03 / THE RACE TO LEAD</span><span
        >COMPETITION → COORDINATION → VERIFICATION</span
      >
    </div><h2>“What if the other<br />side <em>doesn’t stop?”</em></h2><p>
      The strongest objection deserves a real answer. A US–China race framing
      puts reciprocal restraint under pressure. The consequences reach far
      beyond either country.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="race-grid">
    <div class="race-argument">
      <span class="section-label">THE CASE FOR MOVING FIRST</span><p
        class="large-prose"
      >
        A government may see leadership as a source of security, prosperity, and
        influence. Slowing down alone could surrender those advantages without
        reducing a rival’s risks.
      </p><span class="section-label">THE CASE FOR COMMON LIMITS</span><p>
        Racing can impose risks that cross borders. Shared requirements could
        make restraint more credible if participants can verify compliance and
        respond to evasion.
      </p>
    </div><div class="coordination">
      <span class="section-label">CHANGE THE AGREEMENT</span><div
        role="group"
        aria-label="International agreement type"
        class="choice"
      >
        <button data-pact="0" aria-pressed="true">Promises</button><button
          data-pact="1"
          aria-pressed="false">Verified commitments</button
        >
      </div><div class="pact-graphic" aria-hidden="true">
        <span>STATE A</span><div class="pact-lines">
          <i></i><b id="pact-symbol">{pact.symbol}</b><i></i>
        </div><span>STATE B</span>
      </div><h3 id="pact-title">{pact.title}</h3><p id="pact-copy">
        {pact.copy}
      </p><div class="assumption">
        <span id="pact-tag">THE LIMIT</span><p id="pact-limit">{pact.limit}</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div><p class="race-note">
    A conceptual comparison, not a forecast of US or Chinese policy.
    Verification is incomplete; non-participation remains possible. Other
    countries and affected communities need a voice in setting the terms.
  </p><details class="evidence">
    <summary
      ><span>↳ What international oversight can build on</span><span
        class="summary-hint">Existing institutions & their limits</span
      ><span class="plus">+</span></summary
    ><div class="evidence-content">
      <div>
        <h3>Give the institution<br />a job description.</h3><p>
          The <a
            href="https://www.iaea.org/topics/basics-of-iaea-safeguards"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer">IAEA’s safeguards</a
          > rely on technical verification under agreements accepted by states. An
          AI institution would likewise need a basis for access, a defined remit,
          and legitimate authority.
        </p>
      </div><div>
        <p>
          The UN established an <a
            href="https://www.un.org/global-digital-compact/en/ai"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer">AI scientific panel and global dialogue</a
          > in August 2025. Assessment and discussion are valuable functions; they
          do not themselves confer global enforcement powers.
        </p><p>
          This proposal leaves the institutional form open. The test is whether
          it can obtain evidence, act on it, resist pressure, and be held
          accountable itself.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </details>
</section>
